<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Ablations Studies</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents empirical analyses validating our theoretical insights (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 GMixout Error Analysis and Connection to Ensembling ‣ 4 From Mixout To GMixout ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), followed by studies on how the parameter ratio influences the ID–OOD trade-off and how base model choice impacts GMixout. Unless otherwise noted, ablations are conducted on DomainNet Real and Sketch under the same setup as the main experiments; additional results are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Additional ablations ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GMixout validation.</span> To further examine the impact of GMixout hyperparameters (EMA coefficient <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and resampling frequency <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) on the ID–OOD trade-off, we conduct the same analysis as in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c,d) on DomainNet Real (ID), which differs markedly from Sketch, as evident in their zero-shot CLIP performance (65.2 vs. 72.8). In Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 Ablations Studies ‣ 5 Results and Discussion ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we vary <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and vice versa, with sparsity fixed at 0.1. Results show a clear trade-off for <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>: larger values improve OOD accuracy at the expense of ID accuracy. By contrast, higher <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, up until <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math>, consistently improves both ID and OOD performance. Notably, the stable range of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is broader on Sketch (50–300) (Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 Ablations Studies ‣ 5 Results and Discussion ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(right)) than on Real (50–100) (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(d)).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameters budget.</span> Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 Ablations Studies ‣ 5 Results and Discussion ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> compares the effect of the learnable parameter ratio—controlled by rank in LoRA and by mask sparsity in Random Mask, Mixout, and GMixout—on the ID–OOD trade-off. We evaluate ratios from 1% to 10% of learnable parameters. As the ratio increases, all methods trade off OOD for ID, with LoRA being the least sensitive. Across both domains, GMixout consistently yields higher OOD accuracy under any parameter budget while maintaining competitive ID accuracy on Real. On Sketch, it requires lower sparsity to match Random Mask and Mixout. Overall, optimizing about 10% of parameters is a good heuristic for balancing ID and OOD performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:190.8pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06982v1/r_curve.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/130;" width="264" height="130"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>OOD-ID trade-off given variable <math id="S5.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (left) and <math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (right) on DomainNet Real.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F4.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:190.8pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06982v1/pareto_curve.svg" id="S5.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/130;" width="264" height="130"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>OOD-ID trade-off given the variable trainable parameters budget for PEFT methods.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
