<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Benchmark Details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For benchmarking, we use PyTorch v2.7.1 profiling tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paszke et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. All experiments are run on a ViT-B/16 backbone with an NVIDIA RTX 3090 (24 GB VRAM) and batch size 64. We set LoRA rank to <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=64</annotation></semantics></math>, and for Random Masking, Mixout, and GMixout, we use mask sparsity <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, optimizing about 10% of ViT-B/16 total parameters (85M).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Implementation details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train all methods with the AdamW optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov &amp; Hutter, 2019</a>)</cite>, using weight decay <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. Batch size is <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math> for all datasets except ImageNet-1k, where it is <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>. To initialize the linear classifier in CLIP-based ImageNet-1k experiments, we use the prompt templates provided in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al. (2021)</a></cite>. For all other datasets, we adopt the template “a photo of a <span id="A1.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;class&gt;</span>” to obtain the corresponding embeddings from the CLIP text encoder. For each baseline, we tune learning rates from <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1e-2,1e-3,1e-4,1-5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1e-2,1e-3,1e-4,1-5\}</annotation></semantics></math> with a cosine decay scheduler that anneals to <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> after a 1-epoch warmup. Training runs for <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> epochs on ImageNet-1k and DomainNet, and <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> epochs on all other datasets. The best checkpoint is selected using each dataset’s in-domain validation set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gulrajani &amp; Lopez-Paz, 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For long-tail benchmarks, we adopt the <em id="A1.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Logit Adjustment</em> (LA) loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Menon et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, which mitigates head-class bias by adding a label-dependent offset to the logits:</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{LA}(y,f(\bm{x}))=-\log\frac{\exp({g_{y}(\bm{x})+\log\pi_{y})}}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{C}}\exp({f_{y^{\prime}}(\bm{x})+\log\pi_{y^{\prime}})}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{LA}(y,f(\bm{x}))=-\log\frac{\exp({g_{y}(\bm{x})+\log\pi_{y})}}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in\mathcal{C}}\exp({f_{y^{\prime}}(\bm{x})+\log\pi_{y^{\prime}})}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in\Delta_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in\Delta_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> are estimates of the class priors <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(y)</annotation></semantics></math> based on the empirical class frequencies on the training data <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. In a recent study, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2024)</a></cite> observed that starting from CLIP pretrained weights, the LA loss alone is insufficient to achieve strong performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.6 </span>Detail results</h3>

<div id="A1.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show detailed results of our main experiments. Tables <a href="#A1.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ A.6 Detail results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and  <a href="#A1.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ A.6 Detail results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> report the full results on the Real, Sketch, Clipart, and Painting domains of DomainNet, treating each as ID with their corresponding OOD domains. Table <a href="#A1.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ A.6 Detail results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> provides the detailed ID–OOD results corresponding to Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.2 Main Results ‣ 5 Results and Discussion ‣ Revisiting Mixout: An Overlooked Path to Robust Finetuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, using the ImageNet-1k subsampled training sets with 25, 50, 100, and 200 images per class, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T10" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span>OOD accuracy on DomainNet Real and Sketch.</figcaption>
<div id="A1.T10.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:491.6pt;height:134.4pt;vertical-align:-64.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p id="A1.T10.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.T10.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="A1.T10.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="A1.T10.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="A1.T10.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2"><span id="A1.T10.2.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></span>
<span id="A1.T10.2.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="A1.T10.2.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ID</span></span>
